#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065386 :tutorial/content "Suppose that Bob, who is ![](images/ae/ae1e193fbd577e9009d2601d3f65608af0827a16092c4046543cdb1ccddb9f74.svg) tall, stands in front of a ![](images/a7/a78087a5302fc6677b6ef30a50cfb0b170e3f51b10c25027c0427b938ad21148.svg) street light. How fast does the length of Bob's shadow change if he walks *away* from the street light with a speed of ![](images/0c/0cfc62307d856dbbed71500139ffd3427d49977f140a921ec2f53adb8804b61c.svg)\n\n![](images/0b/0b1f84cc2e31c1a7bdcf2663d8d82f4fecab7dfd12990041b2ba7fd0fe4174bf.png)\n\nWe can solve problems like this using three simple steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n![](images/8d/8d80efb54f0c0f025eaec3cf02f4a1a40cb5a47f1dec4d40e1af63c8e4ac047f.png)\n\nIn the above diagram, we have denoted the following:\n\n- ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) is the distance between Bob and the street light\n\n- ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is the length of Bob's shadow\n\nBoth ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) change with time as Bob walks away from the light. In particular, we're told that Bob's velocity is given by\n\n![](images/16/1608be1f575a84b2c86188a18b6a1eec623a97e1d4bf5534e092b8518ba5f837.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/63/632daac2c47092ffbca309417f73fd9d1a3385167c025ca07ca5a19c5632b294.svg) is positive. This is because the distance between Bob and the street light increases as Bob walks away from the light.\n\nOur goal is to calculate ![](images/89/89989044f19d9ed88641aeb7c0c0d0ce32aff15a2088eedbcc8944c9631ba3df.svg) \n\n** Step 2:** Write down an equation that relates ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg)\n\nFrom our simplified diagram, we see that we have two right triangles that share a common angle. Therefore, they are similar by the AA (angle-angle) criterion for similar triangles. \n\nSince the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal. So, we have\n\n![](images/0c/0c73e5832167c46e7cbe124decb8968c19212f433bdc307e7653af2298f88446.svg)\n\n** Step 3:** Differentiate with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nWe use the chain rule to differentiate the above equation with respect to time. This gives\n\n![](images/21/219666475eed9f3f69895ef00a6c843c54c5b40a16b04060b6e4f988373bf694.svg) \n\nFinally, substituting the known value of ![](images/63/632daac2c47092ffbca309417f73fd9d1a3385167c025ca07ca5a19c5632b294.svg) gives\n\n![](images/85/858a9792dd0056bec55459ba9071aee83964102be336cc2fe797ab59f08253be.svg)\n\nTherefore, the length of Bob's shadow increases at a rate of ![](images/94/94cc677a1f2f2b5f1ee25d1290d932b6404e2b840e536c603eb373cc509857c1.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "39868416-9b99-584e-912c-55bb370a34c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
